window.CELPIP_WMODEL = {"W1":{"model":"Subject: Ongoing Late-Night Noise from the Unit Above Mine\n\nDear Ms. Patel,\n\nI am writing to report a noise problem in the apartment directly above mine. For the past two weeks, my upstairs neighbour has played loud music almost every night, often until two in the morning. The bass is strong enough to vibrate my bedroom ceiling, and it has continued even after I knocked politely on my ceiling.\n\nThis situation has affected me considerably. I rarely sleep more than four hours, and I have been arriving at work exhausted. Last Thursday, I made a serious error in a client report because I could not concentrate. I am worried that my health and my job performance will suffer if this continues.\n\nI would appreciate it if you could speak with the neighbour and remind them of the building's quiet hours, which begin at 10 p.m. If the noise continues, I would also ask that you send a written warning. I am happy to meet with you to discuss the details.\n\nThank you for your prompt attention to this matter.\n\nSincerely,\nDaniel Okafor\nApartment 3B","words":184,"why":["The email follows the order of the prompt: problem, impact, then request, so each paragraph has one job.","Specific details (two weeks, two in the morning, four hours of sleep) make the complaint believable.","A formal but calm tone and a clear, reasonable request keep the manager willing to help."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing to report","[detail] for the past two weeks","[impact] This situation has affected me considerably","[request] I would appreciate it if you could","[condition] If the noise continues","[closing] Thank you for your prompt attention to this matter"],"errors":["Writing an angry or threatening message instead of a firm, polite one.","Giving the problem but forgetting a specific request for the manager to act on."]},"W2":{"model":"Subject: Request for Time Off, June 14 to 16\n\nDear Mr. Lopez,\n\nI hope you are well. I am writing to ask for some time off next month. My cousin is getting married in Halifax on Saturday, June 15, and my whole family is travelling there for the weekend. I would like to request Friday, June 14, Saturday, June 15, and Sunday, June 16, as days off.\n\nI know weekends are busy at the store, so I have tried to make the schedule easy to manage. My coworker Amir has agreed to cover my Saturday shift if I take his Wednesday evening shift on June 12. Sofia has also offered to work my Friday and Sunday shifts, and in return I will cover two of her closing shifts the following week. I am also willing to work extra hours before my trip if that would help.\n\nIf this arrangement works for you, I will update the shift-swap sheet right away. Please let me know if you would like me to change anything or if you need more information.\n\nThank you very much for considering my request.\n\nBest regards,\nPriya Nair","words":190,"why":["The reason and exact dates appear early, so the manager understands the request immediately.","The coverage plan names who will work which shift, which shows responsibility.","The closing offers to adjust the plan, which keeps the tone cooperative and polite."],"phrases":["[opening] I hope you are well","[request] I would like to request","[reason] My cousin is getting married","[solution] has agreed to cover my Saturday shift","[offer] I am also willing to work extra hours","[closing] Thank you very much for considering my request"],"errors":["Giving the reason but leaving out exact dates or a plan for covering the shifts.","Sounding like a demand (\"I will be absent\") instead of politely asking for permission."]},"W3":{"model":"I believe the company should offer a subsidized gym membership. My first reason is that it gives employees real choice. Some people prefer swimming, climbing or group classes, and a single on-site room cannot meet all of those interests. A member who lives near a gym in another part of the city can also use it on weekends, so the benefit fits into their whole routine.\n\nMy second reason is that a subsidy is more practical for the company. An on-site fitness room needs space, equipment, cleaning, insurance and regular repairs, and the machines become outdated within a few years. A subsidy, on the other hand, has a predictable monthly cost and can be adjusted whenever the budget changes.\n\nAdmittedly, an on-site room is convenient because staff could exercise during a lunch break without travelling. However, a small room with only a few machines would be crowded at peak hours, and some employees would feel uncomfortable exercising in front of colleagues.\n\nFor these reasons, I am convinced that a subsidized gym membership is the better choice for both the employees and the company.","words":183,"why":["The opinion is stated in the first sentence, and the closing sentence restates it in different words.","Each reason starts with a clear signpost (\"My first reason\", \"My second reason\") and is followed by a concrete example.","The concession paragraph shows balance but still returns to the writer's position with \"However\"."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe the company should","[reason] My first reason is that","[contrast] on the other hand","[concession] Admittedly, an on-site room is convenient","[contrast] However, a small room","[conclusion] For these reasons, I am convinced that"],"errors":["Changing sides halfway through, so the reader cannot tell which option the writer supports.","Listing general benefits (\"it is healthy\") without any specific example."]},"W4":{"model":"In my opinion, in-person courses are better for most students. The main reason is the quality of interaction. In a classroom, a student can ask a quick question, watch a professor demonstrate a technique, and join a spontaneous discussion. In a nursing or engineering lab, for example, hands-on practice with real equipment simply cannot be replaced by a video call.\n\nA second reason is that campus life builds a routine and a network. When students attend class every morning, they meet classmates, form study groups and find mentors. Many of my friends who studied online said they felt isolated and found it hard to stay motivated without a fixed schedule.\n\nOf course, online learning has clear advantages. It is more flexible, and students who work full-time or live far from the college can save travel costs. For these students, online classes may be the only realistic option. Even so, I think flexibility is a convenience, while face-to-face contact is what makes learning deeper and more lasting.\n\nTherefore, I believe the college should keep most of its courses in person and offer online sections only as a supplement.","words":187,"why":["The position is clear from the first sentence, and each paragraph supports it with a different reason.","The counterpoint (flexibility and cost) is acknowledged fairly and then answered, which makes the argument stronger.","Linking words (\"A second reason\", \"Of course\", \"Even so\", \"Therefore\") guide the reader through the logic."],"phrases":["[opinion] In my opinion","[reason] The main reason is","[example] for example","[reason] A second reason is that","[concession] Of course, online learning has clear advantages","[conclusion] Therefore, I believe the college should"],"errors":["Saying both options are good and never committing to one opinion.","Writing one long paragraph with no clear separation between reasons."]},"W-E-01":{"model":"Subject: Faulty Headset Affecting Customer Calls\n\nDear Mr. Chen,\n\nI am writing to report a problem with my headset at workstation 14. Over the past week, the audio has cut out several times during each shift, usually for five to ten seconds at a time. Customers cannot hear me, and I often cannot hear them. I have checked the cable and restarted my computer, but the problem keeps returning.\n\nThis has made it difficult for me to do my job properly. Several callers have had to repeat their concerns, and a few have hung up before I could resolve their issues. As a result, my average call time has increased, and I am worried about missing my weekly targets.\n\nI would be grateful if you could arrange a replacement headset, or at least a technician's visit, by the end of this week. If a replacement is not available, a loaner headset would help in the meantime. I am available to meet at any time during my break.\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nKind regards,\nLeila Hassan\nCustomer Service Representative","words":179,"why":["The problem is described with measurable details (how long, how often, what was already tried).","The impact paragraph connects the fault to customers and targets, which explains why it is urgent.","The request includes both a solution and a timeline, and the tone stays constructive."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing to report a problem with","[detail] Over the past week","[impact] This has made it difficult for me to","[result] As a result","[request] I would be grateful if you could arrange","[solution] in the meantime"],"errors":["Describing the problem as \"broken\" without saying what happens or how often.","Asking for help without suggesting a deadline or a specific fix."]},"W-E-02":{"model":"Subject: Urgent Request for Heating Repairs in Apartment 205\n\nDear Mr. Brennan,\n\nI am writing about the heating system in my apartment, which has not worked properly for the past four days. The radiators stay barely lukewarm, and the indoor temperature has dropped to about 14 degrees. With outdoor temperatures now below freezing, the situation is becoming serious.\n\nMy household has been affected in several ways. I live with my husband and our two-year-old daughter, who has developed a cough after sleeping in a cold room. We have been using space heaters, but they are not safe to leave on overnight, and our electricity use has risen sharply. Sleeping and working from home have both become very uncomfortable.\n\nI respectfully ask that a qualified technician inspect and repair the heating system no later than this Friday. If the repair will take longer, I would appreciate a temporary solution, such as a safe portable heater supplied by the building. Please contact me by phone or email to arrange access to the unit.\n\nThank you for your prompt attention to this urgent matter.\n\nSincerely,\nMarcus Delgado","words":184,"why":["The first paragraph states the duration and conditions, so the manager sees the urgency straight away.","Mentioning a young child gives a real health reason without exaggerating.","A firm deadline is paired with a polite, flexible fallback (a temporary heater)."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing about","[detail] for the past four days","[impact] My household has been affected in several ways","[request] I respectfully ask that","[deadline] no later than this Friday","[closing] Thank you for your prompt attention to this urgent matter"],"errors":["Using vague deadlines such as \"soon\" or \"as soon as possible\" instead of a date.","Becoming rude or threatening, which weakens a formal request."]},"W-E-03":{"model":"Subject: Refund Request for Defective Blender, Order 48213\n\nDear Customer Service Team,\n\nI am writing about a countertop blender that I bought from your website three weeks ago, under order number 48213. I paid $89.99 for it. After only two uses, the motor stopped working completely. I followed the instructions exactly, and I never overfilled the jug or ran the blender longer than recommended. Now, when I plug it in, there is no sound or response at all.\n\nThis problem has been quite inconvenient. I bought the blender to prepare healthy meals for my family, and I have had to go back to buying pre-made food, which costs more. I have also spent time reading the manual and the online troubleshooting guide without success.\n\nI would like a full refund to my original payment method. I can send photographs of the product and my receipt, and I am happy to return the blender at your expense. I would appreciate a reply within five business days.\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nYours sincerely,\nHannah Wells","words":174,"why":["The order number, price and date give the company everything it needs to find the purchase.","The writer shows that the product was used correctly, which protects the claim.","Only one resolution (a refund) is requested, with a reasonable reply deadline."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing about","[detail] After only two uses","[defence] I followed the instructions exactly","[impact] This problem has been quite inconvenient","[request] I would like a full refund to","[closing] Thank you for your assistance"],"errors":["Offering several different solutions (refund, replacement and repair) instead of one clear request.","Leaving out order details, which delays the company's reply."]},"W-E-04":{"model":"Subject: Request to Meet About Amir's Recent Progress\n\nDear Ms. Reyes,\n\nThank you for sending home the latest report card and for your note about Amir. I was concerned to see the drop in his grades, and I would like to understand what is happening.\n\nAt home, I have noticed some changes over the past month. Amir has been quieter than usual at dinner, and he spends much more time alone in his room. He used to talk about his friends and soccer practice, but he rarely mentions them now. When I ask about school, he says everything is \"fine,\" yet he seems unhappy.\n\nI would be very grateful to hear what you have observed in class. Has he seemed more withdrawn during group work or at recess? Have you noticed any changes in his friendships or his concentration? I am sure we both want to support him.\n\nCould we arrange a meeting, either in person or by phone, within the next week or so? I am free after 4 p.m. on most weekdays and can adjust to suit your schedule.\n\nThank you for your care and attention.\n\nWarm regards,\nGrace Mensah","words":192,"why":["Opening with thanks shows the parent is working with the teacher, not blaming her.","Specific observations at home are followed by direct, open questions about school.","The meeting request includes a time frame and flexible availability."],"phrases":["[opening] Thank you for sending home","[concern] I was concerned to see","[observation] I have noticed some changes","[question] I would be very grateful to hear what you have observed","[request] Could we arrange a meeting","[flexibility] can adjust to suit your schedule"],"errors":["Sounding accusatory (\"you did not notice\") instead of curious and cooperative.","Asking for a meeting without suggesting when."]},"W-E-05":{"model":"Subject: Parking in Our Shared Driveway\n\nHi Tom,\n\nI hope you are doing well. I wanted to mention something about our shared driveway, and I am sure we can sort it out easily.\n\nOver the past two weeks, a few of your visitors have parked across the end of the driveway, which has blocked my access to the garage. On Tuesday and again on Friday morning, I had to go door to door to find the drivers, and I was nearly twenty minutes late for work both times. I understand that visitors may not realize where the boundary is, so I am not upset with you.\n\nI have a suggestion that might work for both of us. We could put two small markers along the edge of the driveway so guests know where to park, and I would be glad to pay for them. If you are expecting a larger group, you could also let me know the day before, and I will move my car or park on the street.\n\nThanks so much for your understanding. Please let me know what you think.\n\nBest wishes,\nElena","words":187,"why":["The friendly greeting and \"I am not upset with you\" keep the message non-confrontational.","Two dated examples show the real inconvenience without exaggeration.","The suggested solution is specific and shares the effort, so it is easy to agree to."],"phrases":["[opening] I wanted to mention something about","[detail] Over the past two weeks","[softening] I understand that visitors may not realize","[suggestion] I have a suggestion that might work for both of us","[offer] I would be glad to","[closing] Thanks so much for your understanding"],"errors":["Using a formal or angry tone with a neighbour, which damages the relationship.","Complaining without proposing a practical solution."]},"W-E-06":{"model":"Subject: Request to Cancel Annual Membership, ID 5528\n\nDear Membership Office,\n\nI am writing to request the early cancellation of my annual gym membership. My membership ID is 5528, and I joined in January under the name Carlos Rivera. Unfortunately, my employer has asked me to relocate to Calgary for work, and I will be moving on the first of next month. Since I will no longer live near your location, I will not be able to use the facilities.\n\nI have really enjoyed training at your gym, and I am sorry to leave before my contract ends. Before I proceed, I would like to ask a few questions. What is the process for cancelling early? Do I need to complete a form, or can this be done by email? Also, is any part of my prepaid fee refundable for the unused months, and are there any cancellation charges?\n\nI can provide proof of my relocation, such as a letter from my employer, if that is required. Please confirm my cancellation date in writing so that no further payments are taken from my account.\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nSincerely,\nCarlos Rivera","words":192,"why":["The reason, ID and move date are all in the first paragraph, so the office can act immediately.","The questions are grouped and specific (process, form, refund, charges).","The writer offers proof and asks for written confirmation, which is practical and professional."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing to request","[detail] My membership ID is","[reason] my employer has asked me to relocate","[question] What is the process for cancelling early?","[request] Please confirm my cancellation date in writing","[closing] Thank you for your help"],"errors":["Forgetting to include membership details or the move date.","Asking vague questions such as \"What do I do?\" instead of asking about the refund and the process."]},"W-E-07":{"model":"Subject: Planning Hassan's Retirement Send-Off\n\nHi everyone,\n\nAs many of you know, Hassan is retiring at the end of next month after twenty-two years with our department. To thank him for all his help and kindness, I have volunteered to organize a small farewell gathering, and I would love your help.\n\nI am proposing Friday, the 28th, from 3:00 to 4:30 p.m. in the main meeting room. That should be quiet enough for people to finish their work first and still enjoy some time together.\n\nI need your ideas on two things. For food, I was thinking of sandwiches, fruit and a cake from the bakery down the street. Please tell me about any allergies or dietary needs. For activities, we could share short memories, sign a card and perhaps put together a slideshow of photos. If you have a favourite story about Hassan, please send it to me.\n\nCould you reply by Friday, the 14th, to let me know if you can attend? I will collect a small amount for the gift at the same time.\n\nThanks so much, and I look forward to celebrating with you!\n\nWarm regards,\nNadia","words":191,"why":["The purpose and date come first, so the team knows what the email is for.","The questions about food and activities are separate and easy to answer.","A clear reply deadline and a warm closing balance organization with a friendly tone."],"phrases":["[opening] As many of you know","[purpose] I have volunteered to organize","[proposal] I am proposing Friday, the 28th","[input] I need your ideas on two things","[deadline] Could you reply by Friday, the 14th","[closing] I look forward to celebrating with you"],"errors":["Forgetting the response deadline, so nobody knows when to reply.","Writing in a very formal style for colleagues when the occasion is friendly."]},"W-E-08":{"model":"Subject: Request to Review My Vacation Balance\n\nDear Human Resources Team,\n\nI am writing about a difference I noticed in my vacation balance on the pay portal. The portal currently shows 6 days remaining, but according to my calculation, I should have 9 days left.\n\nMy annual entitlement is 15 days. This year, I took three days in March, two days around the long weekend in May, and one day in August, which makes six days in total. The days I used were all approved by my manager, Ms. Wong, and I still have the approval emails. My records suggest that one additional three-day block may have been recorded twice, but I am not certain, and I would be happy to be corrected if I have made a mistake.\n\nI would be grateful if you could review my record and correct the balance if an error is found. I can forward the approval emails and my notes if they would be helpful. A reply by the end of next week would be appreciated, because I am planning a trip in October.\n\nThank you for your time and assistance.\n\nSincerely,\nJordan Fraser","words":191,"why":["The discrepancy is stated with numbers in the first paragraph, which keeps the email factual.","A clear calculation (15 days, minus 6 used) supports the writer's claim.","Phrases such as \"I am not certain\" and \"I would be happy to be corrected\" avoid sounding accusatory."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing about a difference I noticed","[evidence] according to my calculation","[support] I still have the approval emails","[softening] I would be happy to be corrected","[request] review my record and correct the balance","[closing] Thank you for your time and assistance"],"errors":["Accusing HR of making a mistake instead of asking them to check.","Stating the problem without giving dates or numbers to support it."]},"W-E-09":{"model":"Subject: Request to Exchange a Faulty Laptop, Order 73019\n\nDear Customer Support Team,\n\nI am writing about a laptop I bought from your store two weeks ago (order 73019). Unfortunately, the screen has started to flicker at random, sometimes several times an hour, and the picture becomes unreadable for a few seconds each time. The problem is worse when the laptop is running on battery power.\n\nI have already tried several steps to fix it. I installed the latest graphics driver and all system updates, adjusted the refresh rate, and tested an external monitor, which worked perfectly. Finally, I performed a factory reset, but the flickering returned within an hour. This suggests that the fault lies in the screen hardware, not the software.\n\nBecause the laptop is so new and I use it daily for work, I would like to exchange it for a brand-new unit of the same model. I have kept the box, charger and receipt, and I can bring everything to the store whenever it suits you. Could you please let me know the next steps and how long the exchange will take?\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nSincerely,\nOwen Clarke","words":194,"why":["Troubleshooting steps are listed in order, which proves the writer has done their part before asking for help.","The conclusion that the fault is in the hardware logically supports the request for an exchange.","The tone is polite but assertive, and the closing question moves the process forward."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing about","[problem] the screen has started to flicker at random","[steps] I have already tried several steps","[reasoning] This suggests that the fault lies in","[request] I would like to exchange it for","[next step] Could you please let me know the next steps"],"errors":["Saying \"I tried everything\" without naming the specific steps.","Requesting an exchange without explaining why a repair or software fix is not enough."]},"W-E-10":{"model":"Subject: Request for a Professional Reference\n\nDear Ms. Thompson,\n\nI hope you are doing well. It has been a while since we worked together at Northgate Logistics, and I often think of the things I learned from your guidance.\n\nI am writing to share some news and to ask for a favour. I have applied for the position of operations coordinator at a regional distribution company, and I have reached the final stage of the hiring process. The employer has asked for two professional references.\n\nYou may remember that we worked together for three years, and I managed the weekly scheduling for the warehouse team. Together, we reduced late shipments by nearly 20 per cent in my final year, and you encouraged me to take the lead on training new staff. Those experiences are central to my application.\n\nWould you be willing to act as a reference for me? If so, I would be happy to send you my résumé and the job description to make it easier. I fully understand if your schedule does not allow it.\n\nThank you very much for your time and support.\n\nSincerely,\nAnita Joshi","words":190,"why":["The opening reconnects warmly before making the request, which suits a favour.","Specific shared achievements remind the manager what to say as a reference.","The question is polite and gives the manager an easy way to decline."],"phrases":["[opening] I hope you are doing well","[purpose] I am writing to share some news and to ask for a favour","[reminder] You may remember that we worked together","[achievement] Together, we reduced late shipments","[request] Would you be willing to act as a reference","[courtesy] I fully understand if your schedule does not allow it"],"errors":["Asking for the reference immediately without any greeting or reminder of the past work.","Forgetting to name the position being applied for."]},"W-E-11":{"model":"Subject: Request to Reschedule Tuesday's Appointment\n\nDear Front Desk Team,\n\nI hope you are well. My name is Sam Whitfield, and I have a cleaning and check-up scheduled for next Tuesday at 10:30 a.m. with Dr. Alvarez. Unfortunately, my employer has just scheduled a mandatory training session for that morning, and I am unable to miss it. I am sorry for the short notice and would like to move my appointment.\n\nI have a few times that would suit me later that week. I am available on Wednesday after 2 p.m., on Thursday morning before noon, or on Friday after 3 p.m. If none of these times is open, please tell me what is available, and I will try to adjust my schedule.\n\nCould you please confirm the new appointment by email or phone? I can be reached any time after 5 p.m. Please also let me know if I need to do anything to release the original time slot so that another patient can use it.\n\nThank you for your understanding and help.\n\nKind regards,\nSam Whitfield","words":178,"why":["The reason is brief and the apology is short, which is right for a routine service request.","Three specific alternatives, with times, make it easy for the clinic to say yes.","The final paragraph asks for confirmation and shows consideration for other patients."],"phrases":["[opening] I hope you are well","[reason] I am unable to miss it","[apology] I am sorry for the short notice","[options] I am available on Wednesday after 2 p.m.","[flexibility] I will try to adjust my schedule","[request] Could you please confirm the new appointment"],"errors":["Giving only one alternative time or none at all.","Forgetting to ask for confirmation of the new appointment."]},"W-E-12":{"model":"Subject: Dispute of an Unrecognized $45 Fee on My Chequing Account\n\nDear Customer Service Team,\n\nI am writing to dispute a fee on my most recent chequing account statement. On March 12, a charge of $45.00 appeared under the description \"service adjustment fee.\" I do not recognize this charge, and I believe it was applied in error.\n\nThere are several reasons for my concern. I have not requested any new services or made any changes to my account, and I have always kept my balance above the minimum required, so no overdraft or low-balance fee should apply. I also reviewed my previous six statements, and this fee has never appeared before.\n\nI would like you to investigate the charge and, if it was made in error, refund the full amount to my account. Please also send me a written explanation of what the fee was for. I would appreciate a response by April 5, so that the matter is resolved before my next statement.\n\nI am available by phone or email if you need any further information. Thank you for your attention to this matter.\n\nSincerely,\nMaria Gomez","words":188,"why":["The exact amount, date and description identify the charge immediately.","The reasons are factual and checkable (balance and past statements), so the tone stays formal and unemotional.","The request has three parts: investigate, refund and explain, with a clear deadline."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing to dispute","[detail] a charge of $45.00 appeared under the description","[belief] I believe it was applied in error","[evidence] I also reviewed my previous six statements","[request] I would like you to investigate the charge","[deadline] I would appreciate a response by April 5"],"errors":["Complaining about the bank in general instead of identifying the specific charge.","Requesting an investigation without giving any date for a response."]},"W-E-13":{"model":"Subject: Proposal for a Flexible Start-Time Trial\n\nDear Ms. Alvarez,\n\nI would like to propose an idea that could improve our team's productivity and morale. Currently, everyone starts at 9:00 a.m. I suggest allowing employees to choose their own start time between 7:30 and 10:00 a.m., while keeping core hours from 10:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m. when everyone is available for meetings.\n\nI believe this would benefit the team in two ways. First, people work best at different times of day. Early risers could finish difficult tasks before the office gets busy, and others could avoid the rush-hour traffic that leaves many of us stressed before the day begins. Second, flexible hours would help us attract and keep good employees. Many people value this kind of freedom.\n\nTo test the idea, we could try it for three months with our team only. We could track deadlines, meeting attendance and customer response times, and hold a short survey at the end. If the results are poor, we could simply return to the current schedule.\n\nI would be happy to prepare a one-page plan. Thank you for considering this proposal.\n\nSincerely,\nKevin Tan","words":191,"why":["The idea is explained concretely, with a range of hours and core hours, so the manager can picture it.","Benefits are organized with \"First\" and \"Second\", which makes them easy to follow.","The trial plan limits the risk and shows how success would be measured."],"phrases":["[opening] I would like to propose an idea that","[detail] while keeping core hours","[benefit] I believe this would benefit the team in two ways","[sequence] Second, flexible hours would help us","[plan] To test the idea, we could try it for three months","[offer] I would be happy to prepare a one-page plan"],"errors":["Describing the idea but giving no reasons or only one reason.","Proposing a permanent change instead of a low-risk trial."]},"W-E-14":{"model":"Subject: Feedback on Our Recent Office Event\n\nDear Mr. Rossi,\n\nThank you for providing the catering for our team celebration last Friday. Overall, we were pleased with the quality of the food. The sandwiches and salads were fresh and well presented, and several colleagues complimented the desserts.\n\nUnfortunately, two problems affected the event. The delivery arrived more than an hour late, so our guests waited with nothing to eat and we had to delay the start of the lunch. In addition, several items from our order were missing, including the vegetarian platter and the coffee service, which meant that some people did not have a proper meal.\n\nWe would like to use your services again, and I hope the following suggestions will help. First, a confirmation call the day before an event would make sure that the order and the delivery time are correct. Second, a checklist at the time of delivery would ensure that nothing is left out. If these steps were in place, I am confident that our next event would go smoothly.\n\nI would appreciate hearing how you plan to address these issues. Thank you for your time.\n\nSincerely,\nLauren Boyd","words":194,"why":["Starting with real praise makes the criticism easier to accept.","The problems are specific (an hour late, vegetarian platter, coffee service) and the effect on guests is explained.","Practical suggestions show that the aim is improvement, not just complaint."],"phrases":["[opening] Thank you for providing","[praise] Overall, we were pleased with","[contrast] Unfortunately, two problems affected the event","[addition] In addition, several items from our order were missing","[suggestion] I hope the following suggestions will help","[closing] I would appreciate hearing how you plan to address"],"errors":["Writing only complaints and forgetting to mention what went well.","Saying the service was \"bad\" without naming the specific problems."]},"W-E-15":{"model":"Subject: Broken Streetlight on Maple Court\n\nDear Public Works Department,\n\nI am writing to report a streetlight that has been out for more than three weeks. It is the lamp post in front of 42 Maple Court, near the corner of Maple Court and Birch Avenue. I first noticed the problem on the 3rd, and it has not been repaired since.\n\nThis is causing a real safety concern for residents. The block is very dark after sunset, and the nearest working light is at the end of the street. Several neighbours, including seniors and parents with strollers, have told me that they feel unsafe walking home after dark. There is also a risk of someone tripping on the uneven sidewalk or of a driver not seeing a pedestrian.\n\nI respectfully ask that the streetlight be repaired as soon as possible, ideally within the next week. If you need further details, the pole number is printed on a small metal plate. I would also appreciate a confirmation once the work has been scheduled.\n\nThank you for your attention to this matter.\n\nSincerely,\nRavi Menon","words":183,"why":["An exact address and nearest intersection let the department find the problem quickly.","The safety concern is explained through who is affected and what could happen.","The request is direct and includes a time frame, in a formal and civic tone."],"phrases":["[opening] I am writing to report","[location] near the corner of Maple Court and Birch Avenue","[concern] This is causing a real safety concern for residents","[evidence] have told me that they feel unsafe walking home after dark","[request] I respectfully ask that the streetlight be repaired","[closing] Thank you for your attention to this matter"],"errors":["Naming only the street, not the exact location of the problem.","Writing \"fix it now\" in a demanding tone rather than a respectful, direct request."]},"W-S-01":{"model":"I believe the company should provide a subsidized public transit pass. First, it is more affordable and fairer. Parking spaces are expensive to build and maintain, and they only help the employees who drive. A transit pass supports everyone who is willing to use it, including junior staff who may not own a car. For an employee who spends $150 a month on gas and parking, a subsidized pass could save hundreds of dollars a year.\n\nSecond, public transit is better for the environment and for the city. Each person who takes a bus or train means one less car on the road, which reduces traffic and pollution. A company that encourages cleaner commuting also improves its public image.\n\nAdmittedly, free parking is more convenient for workers who live in areas with poor transit service. Even so, such cases are less common in a large city, and the company could offer those workers a smaller allowance for other commuting costs.\n\nFor these reasons, I am confident that the subsidized transit pass is the better benefit.","words":175,"why":["The opinion is clear in the first sentence, and each reason begins with a signpost word.","Concrete numbers ($150 a month) make the cost argument more convincing.","The alternative is acknowledged and answered with a practical compromise."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe the company should provide","[reason] First, it is more affordable and fairer","[example] For an employee who spends $150 a month","[reason] Second, public transit is better for","[concession] Admittedly, free parking is more convenient","[conclusion] For these reasons, I am confident that"],"errors":["Choosing one option but spending most of the answer praising the other.","Giving reasons without any example, such as a cost or a situation."]},"W-S-02":{"model":"I think the city should add more frequent bus routes. The main reason is accessibility. Buses serve a far wider group of people than bike lanes do, including seniors, students, people with disabilities and workers who cannot afford a car. A parent who must take two children to school and daycare before work cannot easily do so by bicycle, but a reliable bus every ten minutes would make the trip possible.\n\nMy second reason is year-round usefulness. In Canadian winters, snow and freezing temperatures make cycling unrealistic for many months, whereas buses run in almost any weather. More frequent service also reduces waiting time, which is a common complaint among riders. When buses are reliable, more people leave their cars at home, and traffic improves for everyone.\n\nProtected cycling lanes do improve safety and encourage exercise, and I support them in the long term. However, they mainly benefit healthy, confident riders, and building them along busy roads is very costly.\n\nFor these reasons, I believe more frequent bus routes are the wiser investment.","words":173,"why":["The first reason (accessibility) and second reason (year-round use) are different, so the argument feels complete.","A realistic example, a parent with two children, makes the idea easy to picture.","The final paragraph grants the value of cycling lanes before restating the choice."],"phrases":["[opinion] I think the city should add","[reason] The main reason is accessibility","[contrast] whereas buses run in almost any weather","[reason] My second reason is","[concession] I support them in the long term. However,","[conclusion] I believe more frequent bus routes are the wiser investment"],"errors":["Mentioning an option's benefit in a single word (\"safe\") without explaining it.","Ending abruptly with no final sentence that restates the opinion."]},"W-S-03":{"model":"I think the company should switch to a four-day workweek. My first reason is that a longer weekend gives employees real time to rest and manage their lives. With three days off, a parent can attend school events, book medical appointments and spend time with family without using vacation days. Many employers that have tested shorter weeks report similar output, because rested workers are more focused and make fewer mistakes.\n\nSecond, a four-day week helps everyone equally. Working from home suits people who have a quiet space and a good internet connection, but it can leave others feeling isolated or struggling to separate work from home life. Employees in small apartments or with young children at home may find it hard to concentrate. A shorter week offers the same benefit to all staff, and the team can still meet in person.\n\nOf course, working from home saves commuting time and money. Even so, I believe a fixed extra day off is a stronger improvement to work-life balance.\n\nTherefore, I recommend the four-day workweek.","words":173,"why":["The writer links each reason back to work-life balance, which is the real topic of the question.","Examples are personal and relatable (a parent, a small apartment).","\"Of course\" and \"Even so\" present the other option fairly and then answer it."],"phrases":["[opinion] I think the company should switch to","[reason] My first reason is that","[example] a parent can attend school events","[reason] Second, a four-day week helps everyone equally","[concession] Of course, working from home saves","[conclusion] Therefore, I recommend"],"errors":["Choosing both options (\"a four-day week and working from home\") instead of one.","Repeating the same reason in different words instead of giving a second idea."]},"W-S-04":{"model":"I believe the city should build the larger public library. First, a library serves almost every age group. Children attend story time and homework help, students use quiet study rooms, newcomers take free language classes, and seniors borrow books and join community talks. A sports complex mainly serves people who are already active or who play organized sports.\n\nSecond, a library offers resources that many residents cannot afford at home. Not every family has a reliable computer, a printer or a quiet place to study. Meeting rooms can also be booked by small businesses and community groups at little or no cost, so the building supports learning and local enterprise. A friend of mine prepared for her licensing exam almost entirely in her local library.\n\nAdmittedly, a sports complex would encourage fitness and give teenagers a place to meet. However, many neighbourhoods already have school gyms, parks and private fitness clubs, while quiet, free learning spaces are much harder to find.\n\nIn conclusion, a larger library is the better investment for the whole community.","words":174,"why":["The first reason lists the age groups served, which directly addresses the checklist about community needs.","The second reason adds a personal example, which makes the argument specific.","The concession names a real benefit of the sports complex before explaining why the library still wins."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe the city should build","[reason] First, a library serves almost every age group","[reason] Second, a library offers resources that","[example] A friend of mine prepared for","[concession] Admittedly, a sports complex would encourage","[conclusion] In conclusion, a larger library is the better investment"],"errors":["Using general words such as \"good for people\" without naming who benefits.","Forgetting to compare the choice with the other option at all."]},"W-S-05":{"model":"In my view, practical, hands-on training is more valuable for students. The first reason is career readiness. Employers want graduates who can do the job from the first week. A student who has spent a semester in a co-op placement, a lab or a workshop already knows the tools, the teamwork and the pace of a real workplace. For example, a business graduate who has built a real budget or marketing plan can answer interview questions with confidence.\n\nThe second reason is that practice makes learning stick. People usually remember what they have done much better than what they have only heard in a lecture. When a student must solve a real problem, they have to apply theory, so the knowledge becomes useful rather than just memorized.\n\nI do agree that theoretical knowledge gives students a strong foundation and critical thinking skills, and it matters for research careers. However, theory is best learned through application, and most students attend university to prepare for work.\n\nFor these reasons, I think the university should focus more on hands-on job training.","words":178,"why":["The opening phrase marks an opinion, and the first reason is introduced with a clear label.","Examples (co-op placement, a real budget) are practical and believable.","The writer agrees partly with the opposite view and then explains why it is less important."],"phrases":["[opinion] In my view","[reason] The first reason is career readiness","[example] For example, a business graduate who","[reason] The second reason is that practice makes learning stick","[concession] I do agree that theoretical knowledge gives","[conclusion] For these reasons, I think the university should"],"errors":["Using \"I think\" in every sentence instead of varying the way opinions are introduced.","Writing about job training in general rather than about students' learning."]},"W-S-06":{"model":"I think the city should charge a small fee for each plastic bag. My main reason is that a fee changes behaviour without causing hardship. When shoppers have to pay even ten cents, most remember to bring their own reusable bags. Many cities that have introduced such fees have seen a large drop in bag use, so the environmental benefit can be close to that of a ban.\n\nMy second reason is flexibility and fairness. Sometimes people forget their bags or make an unplanned stop on the way home, and a ban would leave them with nowhere to carry groceries or force them to buy a heavier, more expensive bag. A fee lets them choose, and the money collected can fund recycling programs or community clean-ups. Small shops would also find a fee easier to follow than a complete ban.\n\nSupporters of a ban argue that it is simpler and more effective. That is partly true, but a ban can push stores toward thicker plastic or paper bags, which may be worse for the environment.\n\nFor these reasons, I believe a small fee is the wiser policy.","words":187,"why":["Each reason has a short explanation and a concrete example (ten cents, forgotten bags).","The counterpoint is introduced with \"Supporters of a ban argue\", which is a natural way to present the other side.","The final sentence repeats the opinion with new wording, as the checklist requires."],"phrases":["[opinion] I think the city should charge","[reason] My main reason is that","[reason] My second reason is flexibility and fairness","[example] Sometimes people forget their bags","[counter] Supporters of a ban argue that","[conclusion] I believe a small fee is the wiser policy"],"errors":["Making claims about the environment without saying how the policy changes behaviour.","Copying the question wording in the opinion and conclusion instead of rephrasing."]},"W-S-07":{"model":"I believe the company should hold its annual conference in person. The most important reason is networking. At a hotel venue, colleagues from different offices can meet over coffee, share ideas after a presentation and build relationships that last all year. In a video call, people tend to join, listen and leave, and those casual conversations rarely happen. For example, a new employee can introduce themselves to senior managers during lunch, which is almost impossible online.\n\nMy second reason is attention. When employees attend in person, they are away from their usual desks, so they give the sessions their full focus. At home, there are emails, messages and interruptions, and many people would check their phones during presentations.\n\nI recognize that a virtual conference is cheaper and easier for employees with disabilities or family responsibilities. To address this, the company could provide travel support and record the sessions for those who cannot attend.\n\nIn conclusion, an in-person conference would give the company stronger connections, and I recommend this format.","words":169,"why":["The strongest reason (networking) comes first, and a specific example supports it.","The concession on cost and accessibility is followed by a practical solution rather than being ignored.","The last sentence restates the choice and links it to a benefit for the company."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe the company should hold","[reason] The most important reason is networking","[example] For example, a new employee can introduce themselves","[reason] My second reason is attention","[concession] I recognize that a virtual conference is","[conclusion] In conclusion, an in-person conference would give"],"errors":["Writing about the options without saying which one the company should choose.","Raising the cost of the other option but offering no way to deal with it."]},"W-S-08":{"model":"In general, I think renting a smaller apartment close to work is the better option for a newcomer. First, it saves both time and money. A daily commute of ninety minutes adds up to about seven hours a week, which is almost a full working day. Fuel, parking and car maintenance also add up, and a newcomer may not have a car yet. Living near work removes these costs and leaves more money for savings.\n\nSecond, it supports quality of life and a smoother settlement. With the time saved, a newcomer can attend language classes, meet people in the community and spend evenings with family instead of sitting in traffic. Renting also gives flexibility. If the first job does not work out, it is easy to move, whereas selling a house in the suburbs can be slow and expensive.\n\nI understand that a bigger home is attractive, especially for a family, and owning property can build wealth. However, a large mortgage combined with a long commute creates stress during the most uncertain stage of life in a new country.\n\nFor these reasons, option A is the more sensible choice.","words":189,"why":["The time calculation (ninety minutes a day, about seven hours a week) turns a general idea into a concrete one.","The reasons cover money, time and quality of life, which matches the checklist.","The alternative is treated respectfully and then outweighed with \"However\"."],"phrases":["[opinion] In general, I think renting a smaller apartment","[reason] First, it saves both time and money","[example] A daily commute of ninety minutes adds up to","[reason] Second, it supports quality of life","[concession] I understand that a bigger home is attractive","[conclusion] For these reasons, option A is the more sensible choice"],"errors":["Ignoring the newcomer's situation and writing only about housing in general.","Listing costs without comparing the two options."]},"W-S-09":{"model":"I believe the company should open an on-site health clinic. The first reason is convenience. Employees often avoid seeing a doctor because it means waiting weeks for an appointment and taking half a day off work. With an on-site clinic, a worker could get a flu shot, a blood pressure check or advice about a minor injury in twenty minutes during the workday. Small problems would be treated before they become serious.\n\nThe second reason is the benefit for the company. Fewer long absences and faster recovery from minor illnesses mean fewer lost working days. A clinic can also run vaccination days and wellness programs that reach the whole workplace, which builds a healthier culture.\n\nSubsidizing insurance premiums is certainly helpful, since it lowers medical costs and can cover specialists, dental care and prescriptions. However, insurance only helps if people can actually find time to see a provider, and in Canada many basic medical services are already publicly covered.\n\nFor these reasons, an on-site clinic is the better choice.","words":169,"why":["The first reason focuses on employees and the second on the company, so the argument covers both sides.","Specific services (flu shot, blood pressure check) make \"convenience\" concrete.","The alternative is acknowledged fairly and then answered with a Canadian context point."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe the company should open","[reason] The first reason is convenience","[example] a worker could get a flu shot","[reason] The second reason is the benefit for the company","[concession] Subsidizing insurance premiums is certainly helpful","[contrast] However, insurance only helps if"],"errors":["Describing health benefits in general without explaining why the chosen option is better.","Forgetting to compare the chosen option with the alternative."]},"W-S-10":{"model":"I think the company should switch to instant messaging for daily communication. The first reason is speed. A quick question, such as which file to use, can be answered in a minute in a chat, while an email may sit in an inbox for hours. When teams work to deadlines, those hours matter. For example, a project group can solve a small problem in a short thread instead of exchanging six emails.\n\nThe second reason is that messaging is clearer and less cluttered. Messages are organized by channel and topic, so people can follow the whole conversation instead of digging through long email chains with many replies and attachments. Searching for a past decision is also easy.\n\nEmail still has advantages. It is better for formal announcements, contracts and messages to people outside the company, and it creates a clear record. For that reason, the company should keep email for those purposes. Even so, most daily communication is short and informal, and chat suits it better.\n\nIn summary, instant messaging should become the main communication tool.","words":176,"why":["The key idea (speed) is supported with a believable work example.","The writer suggests keeping email for formal use, which shows balanced judgment.","\"In summary\" signals the end and restates the position in new words."],"phrases":["[opinion] I think the company should switch to","[reason] The first reason is speed","[example] can be answered in a minute","[reason] The second reason is that messaging is clearer","[concession] Email still has advantages","[conclusion] In summary, instant messaging should become"],"errors":["Saying chat is \"modern\" or \"easy\" without showing how it helps at work.","Writing a very casual answer, with slang, when the survey is for a workplace."]},"W-S-11":{"model":"I believe the company should choose private cubicles. The main reason is focus. Many jobs, such as writing reports, analyzing data or speaking with clients on the phone, need concentration. In an open-plan office, every conversation, ringing phone and passing coworker is a distraction, and in my experience it takes several minutes to refocus after each interruption. A cubicle gives each person a quiet space to finish deep work.\n\nA second reason is privacy and comfort. Employees can keep personal items, take a confidential call or discuss a performance review without being overheard. Shared desks also mean that people have no permanent place, which can feel unsettled and tiring.\n\nOpen-plan layouts do encourage collaboration, and spontaneous chats can spark ideas. However, the company can solve this by creating a few shared meeting areas and a lounge where teams can talk freely. That way, employees get collaboration when they want it and quiet when they need it.\n\nFor these reasons, private cubicles are the better layout.","words":165,"why":["The reason about focus is supported with job examples and a clear explanation of the problem.","The counterpoint (collaboration) is answered with a practical design solution.","The last line is short and restates the choice."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe the company should choose","[reason] The main reason is focus","[example] such as writing reports, analyzing data","[reason] A second reason is privacy and comfort","[concession] Open-plan layouts do encourage collaboration","[solution] That way, employees get collaboration when they want it"],"errors":["Using the words \"noise\" and \"privacy\" without describing a real work situation.","Describing both layouts neutrally and never stating a preference."]},"W-S-12":{"model":"I think the city should prioritize expanding and improving public parks. First, parks improve health for people of all ages and incomes. A free green space lets children play, adults jog or walk, and seniors stay active, without the cost of a gym membership. Regular time outdoors also reduces stress, which is valuable in a busy city. In my neighbourhood, a new park trail quickly became crowded with families every weekend.\n\nSecond, parks help the environment. Trees cool the air in summer, absorb rainwater and reduce flooding, and they provide habitat for birds and pollinators. These benefits are difficult to get from almost any other type of spending.\n\nPublic transit is certainly important. It helps people reach jobs and reduces traffic, and I would not say it deserves less attention in general. However, a transit system already exists in most cities, while parks are often the first thing lost when land is developed. Extra funding can therefore make a bigger difference to parks.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that investing in parks is the best use of the new funds.","words":179,"why":["Both reasons (health and environment) are broad and clearly different from each other.","The personal example about a park trail makes the point concrete.","\"However\" turns the concession into support for the writer's own choice."],"phrases":["[opinion] I think the city should prioritize","[reason] First, parks improve health for people of all ages","[example] In my neighbourhood, a new park trail","[reason] Second, parks help the environment","[concession] Public transit is certainly important","[conclusion] In conclusion, I believe that investing in parks"],"errors":["Mixing up the two options and arguing for transit in the middle of a parks answer.","Using only general statements such as \"parks are nice\" with no specific benefit."]},"W-S-13":{"model":"I believe shortening the summer break is the better approach. My first reason is student fatigue. Young children have limited attention, and a longer day, particularly in the late afternoon, leads to restlessness and poor concentration. Adding extra hours every day would also leave less time for homework, sports and sleep. Spreading the extra instruction across more weeks keeps each day at a manageable length.\n\nMy second reason is that a long summer break causes students to forget what they have learned. Teachers often spend the first weeks of September reviewing last year's material. A shorter break, for example four weeks instead of eight, would reduce that loss and give students more continuous learning.\n\nI recognize that families depend on the current calendar for vacations and summer camps, and a shorter break might cause some inconvenience. However, a longer school day would also disrupt family schedules, such as after-school care and pickup times, every single day of the year.\n\nFor these reasons, shortening the summer break is the more sensible choice.","words":171,"why":["Both reasons link directly to learning: fatigue during the day and forgetting over the summer.","The example of four weeks instead of eight makes the proposal specific.","The family-schedule counterpoint is turned around to support the writer's choice."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe shortening the summer break is the better approach","[reason] My first reason is student fatigue","[example] for example four weeks instead of eight","[reason] My second reason is that","[concession] I recognize that families depend on","[conclusion] For these reasons, shortening the summer break is the more sensible choice"],"errors":["Treating the school-day and summer-break options as the same idea.","Discussing only the effect on students and ignoring families or teachers."]},"W-S-14":{"model":"I believe the company should invest in staff training and development. The first reason is long-term value. Software can become outdated or be replaced in a few years, but the skills employees gain stay with the team. A well-trained analyst can use many tools, solve unexpected problems and teach colleagues, which improves efficiency across the company.\n\nSecond, training improves morale and retention. Employees who feel that the company invests in them are more likely to stay, and replacing a skilled worker is expensive. For example, a customer service team that learns better problem-solving techniques can resolve issues faster, which pleases customers directly.\n\nAI automation can undoubtedly handle repetitive tasks quickly, and it may reduce costs in areas such as data entry. Nevertheless, new software requires people who understand it, and without proper training, employees may use it badly or resist it. In fact, training would help staff get the most from any future technology.\n\nIn conclusion, I think investing in people is the wiser choice for lasting efficiency.","words":168,"why":["The argument contrasts something that expires (software) with something that stays (skills), which is easy to remember.","The concession on AI is specific (data entry) and then linked back to the need for training.","Words like \"Nevertheless\" and \"In fact\" show control of contrast and emphasis."],"phrases":["[opinion] I believe the company should invest in","[reason] The first reason is long-term value","[example] For example, a customer service team that","[concession] AI automation can undoubtedly handle","[contrast] Nevertheless, new software requires people who","[conclusion] In conclusion, I think investing in people is"],"errors":["Arguing that AI is bad in general instead of comparing the two investments.","Giving examples that do not match the option chosen."]},"W-S-15":{"model":"I think the office should switch to reusable dishware and cutlery. First, it is cheaper over time. Compostable plates and forks must be bought again every week, and the cost grows with the number of employees. A set of plates, mugs and cutlery is a one-time purchase that could last for years.\n\nSecond, reusable items create less waste overall. Compostable products sound environmentally friendly, but they only break down properly in industrial composting facilities. If the building has no such program, they end up in the garbage anyway, and producing them still uses energy and materials. Reusable dishware avoids that problem entirely.\n\nAdmittedly, washing dishes takes effort, and a shared kitchen can become messy if people leave cups in the sink. This can be managed with a dishwasher, a simple cleaning schedule and a clear policy that everyone washes their own items.\n\nIn my view, the small amount of cleaning is worth the savings and the reduced waste, so reusable dishware is the better choice for the office.","words":168,"why":["Cost and environmental impact are both covered, and each has a specific supporting detail.","The main weakness of the chosen option (cleaning) is raised honestly and solved.","The conclusion weighs the trade-off in one sentence, which feels thoughtful."],"phrases":["[opinion] I think the office should switch to","[reason] First, it is cheaper over time","[reason] Second, reusable items create less waste overall","[concession] Admittedly, washing dishes takes effort","[solution] This can be managed with","[conclusion] In my view, the small amount of cleaning is worth"],"errors":["Saying \"compostable is better because it is natural\" without explaining how it works in practice.","Ignoring the practical problem of cleaning in a shared kitchen."]}};
